  {
    "description": "Add a TRUE 3D object (WebGL/Three.js under the hood) to a composition — it renders with real depth and rotates so every side is visible (unlike the 2.5D layers). \`object\` is one of box | rounded-box | sphere | cylinder | cone | torus | icosahedron | plane | phone (a full iPhone-style device with screen + camera plateau) | model (load ANY .glb/.gltf via modelUrl — auto-centered and scaled to fit) | text3d (extruded 3D text via \`text\`, with \`extrude\` depth). Declarative — you describe it, the engine does the WebGL. Optional: name, x, y (pixel center), width, height (render bounds), size/depth/aspect/cornerRadius (geometry), color, metalness (0..1), roughness (0..1), materialKind (physical|basic), emissive, mapAssetId (an image asset id to texture onto the phone screen, e.g. a wallpaper from import_image_layer), rotationX/Y/Z (initial degrees), fov, cameraDistance, environment (studio|dark|none — studio gives realistic reflections on metal/glass), groundShadow (true drops a soft contact shadow under the object), startTime, duration. Animate the spin with animate_layer property 'transform.rotation.y' (or .x/.z) — e.g. 0→360 over 4s shows the whole device turning in 3D. ZOOM/DOLLY: keyframe 'transform.position.z' — +1 pushes the camera 2x closer (zoom in), +2 = 4x, negative pulls back. Combine a position.z 0→1.5 ramp with a rotation.y tilt for a cinematic push-in.",
